00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.230 align:start position:0%
 
hello everyone  bonjour tour  i am so honored to  be joining you here today at cfb trenton  which is on the traditional territory of  the mohawks of the bay of quinte  also known  as kanye kahaka community of the taienta  naga  this is my first visit to this community  since being appointed as the minister of  national defense  and i truly appreciate  the  opportunity  to learn more about how the canadian  armed forces members stationed here  are keeping canada and north america  safe  and helping to uphold our collective  security including the work that they  are doing in response to the crisis in  ukraine  since putin launched his full-scale  invasion of ukraine two months ago  cfp trenton has served  as the departure point for much of the  military aid that canada has been  providing to ukraine  to fight and win this war  mal grey  to help address the growing crisis at  the border between poland and ukraine  i am announcing today the deployment of  up to 150  canadian armed forces personnel  with approximately 100 personnel in the  immediate term who will assist poland's  efforts to support  and care  for ukrainians  fleeing violence  the majority  will  come from edmonton and kingston  and through here in trenton in the  coming days  [Music]  now over the next few months our  military will provide support across  three lines of effort  first we will be deploying armed forces  members to reception centers  across poland to assist with the  immediate care and processing of  ukrainian refugees  this is the largest component of our  deployment to poland  our people will provide general support  spiritual services and limited medical  care enabled by ukrainian speaking caf  personnel  second  we will deploy a number of members in  support of a polish-led humanitarian  assistance task force  we will work with military members and  other government representatives from  canada  from poland and from other allies to  synchronize efforts  from more than 100 multinational  humanitarian agencies to ensure  that ukrainians fleeing the war  receive the best support possible  and third we will assist immigration  refugees and citizenship canada by  acting as a liaison  with the polish defense forces as ircc  works so that thousands more ukrainians  can continue to resettle in this country  contribute  these efforts are being carried out as  part of operation reassurance  canada's contribution to nato's  assurance and deterrence measures in  central and eastern europe which we  recently expanded and extended over the  past few months  through operation reassurance and the  international response to the war in  ukraine  we have shown how strong and effective  we can be  when we work on a united front  it is more important than ever for  canada to stay engaged with our allies  and partners  and through budget 2022 we have  committed another half billion dollars  worth of military aid  to ukraine and that is on top of the 110  million dollars that we have pledged  thus far so that our ukrainian friends  have the equipment that they need to  keep fighting this war and  [Music]  as this crisis continues to unfold we  will keep working with the international  community to respond effectively  and we will keep ensuring that our  military has what it needs to protect  and to defend canada  to this end  through budget 2022 we announced more  than eight billion dollars in new  funding for our military and the  communications security establishment  we will also undertake a review of  defense policy to ensure that we remain  ready  to meet current and emerging threats  here at home and around the world  in many ways the world is a much less  safe place than it was two years ago  and certainly two months ago  and we must take stock and take action  in order to remain a valuable reliable  ally and partner for decades to come  to conclude i cannot imagine  the heartbreak felt by millions of  ukrainians who are forced to flee  putin's shelling and bombing  the heartbreak of thousands of canadians  with loved ones who remain in ukraine  now  but in the face of unspeakable acts of  violence carried out against innocent  civilians  and of despicable acts  and attacks on schools apartment  buildings train stations in kiev kharkiv  boucha  and of tragic desperation  canadians and our canadian armed forces  will continue to step up  and offer a safe haven and to welcome  with open arms the people of ukraine  our friends  thank you merci nige  i can answer questions if you like thank  you minister we'll now start the  question and answer period from  journalists right here merci beaucoup  uh journalists will have one question  and one follow-up and uh please state  your name and outlet  david aiken global news good morning  minister  um  i wonder if you could maybe speak to the  genesis of this particular mission um  whether it was through a request from  polish authorities i know the prime  minister has spoken quite often to uh  ukraine's prime minister ukraine's  president um did we offer can you give  us a sense of how this mission sort of  came together  well i'll start with the basic point  that canada stands with ukraine we've  been one of the lead countries within  nato in terms of support for ukraine and  we will continue to provide  comprehensive humanitarian  economic and military aid in terms of  today's announcement  the prime minister and i have been in  close touch with our counterparts in  poland as well as with the nato alliance  writ large and we are committing these  additional  resources under operation reassurance  which is uh our mission in support of  nato's eastern flank  thank you  there are some reports i think from the  csc that uh the russians part of the  russian disinformation campaign is to  generate images that seemed to purport  to show canadian forces actively engaged  in combat  in  i guess in ukraine in the theater  thinking about that i wonder if you can  comment does that raise the threat level  for some of the folks who are about to  deploy or for our folks already overseas  right now has has the threat level for  our forces changed i know you can't get  into too much detail on that can you  speak to that in general terms  from the very beginning  we have been  unified as a nato alliance that we will  do whatever is needed to ensure  that we are supporting ukraine and also  that we are making our respective  contributions on the ground to the nato  alliance  and this mission in poland is part of  that commitment  and we  very much are aware of article 5 of the  washington treaty  that an attack on one is an attack on  all  the threat level of course is  one that we are constantly watching and  we will continue to do so  at the current time we are confident  that this is a mission that should be  undertaken in the interests of  humanitarian aid in the interests of  aiding our polish allies and in the  interests of the nato alliance writ  large  as refugees  are fleeing ukraine and this  humanitarian support is needed in poland  immediately  thank you merci next question  hi minister tom perry with cbc you  mentioned that there's going to be  ukrainian speakers going on this mission  i'm wondering if you can tell me um how  many  of  the people we're sending will be  ukrainian speakers what they'll be doing  and as a second part of this question  you mentioned in the budget of the  budget talked about 500 million dollars  in further military aid for ukraine  uh when do you think you're going to be  getting some more detail on that and if  i can get you to answer in english and  french that'd be great  thank you so much for the question  on the first question regarding  ukrainian speakers i will  turn  uh to  ryan uh but  on the second question i have to admit i  couldn't quite hear you um can you speak  sure it was about the about the 500  million dollars in uh weapons aid for  ukraine announced in the budget when can  we expect further details about what  that's going to be  okay i'll start with that and then i'll  hand over the mic on ukrainian speakers  in the  forces  the budget which came down just a week  ago today committed 500 million dollars  in military aid to ukraine that is on  top of the 110 million dollars of aid  already that our government has  committed  this age should be conceived in  various categories which is how i think  about it the first category is calf  inventory which we have largely drawn  upon for the existing aid that we are  sending the second category is  procurements from suppliers we are  actively engaged with  a number of suppliers regarding  additional procurements and i will have  more information to say about the  those discussions soon  i will say that they follow the type of  negotiation and contract that we entered  into for the cameras for drones that we  announced  a few weeks ago  we were asked by ukraine directly to  supply cameras for these drones within  days we had a contract in place and  cameras  sent over to ukraine that is the type of  effort we will continue to do under that  second category and the third category  of aid that we are examining is in  conjunction with our allies  working together collaboratively with  our allies in terms of possibly pooling  our aid so that we can send over aid as  efficiently  as possible as a collective and in that  vein we did have a donors conference  with which was hosted by the united  kingdom recently and  we are very much aligned as  nato allies to ensure that we are  working efficiently as possible given  the urgency of the situation and the  fact that uh minister resnickoff is in  touch with me  almost daily in terms of what is needed  for ukraine and that's what i'm working  to fulfill in terms of the list can i  get you just repeat that briefly in  french  on the uh just  convo  deja  [Music]  thank you minister  so i understand the question correctly  you're asking how many ukrainian  speakers we'll be sending uh over to  theater is that correct sir exactly how  many and i guess what role are they  going to be playing in the operation so  i will answer the question by saying  that we have a very diverse force in the  canadian armed forces and with that  comes many different people with  proficiency in different languages as  far as the number of folks that we are  sending over to the operation i can't  answer that for you because i don't have  those details in front of me and as far  as how they will be employed i think  when we send anyone to these different  operations in different locations around  the world we try to ensure that they  have the linguistic capability such that  we can integrate better with the folks  who are trying to help thank you  and minister just as a follow-up then  a review into the  rapid release of former navy commander  danny croucher found  the military violated its own policy how  did that happen and how will you prevent  it from happening again  sorry  every single day um i continue to work  with general air and leaders across the  canadian armed forces to build a  military where everyone feels safe  protected and respected and this is  crucial to the canadian armed forces  mission to serve and protect canadians  i am aware  that there is  a  situation  in terms of our policy  where members are alleged to have  violated professional standards  but are legally entitled to a release  and i believe that is the situation that  you are raising in your question  i will say  that our processes need to be better  aligned so that victims and survivors do  feel safe respected and protected at  work  we have the cpcc  and jenny calhoun that is working on a  number of initiatives  to ensure that members feel safe  protected and respected at work we are  also awaiting the report  of madame arbor  that hopefully will speak to this issue  thank you  can i just get you to repeat that in  french please  so  mercy  thank you merci do you have any other  questions  all right seeing none this concludes  today's press conference

